LOGBOOK.md:181:- GATE: A reviewer-owned unfiltered streak passed 16 consecutive 427-test runs, including historical hang positions 13 and 16, then run 17 exited 1 because `channelOperationPressureIsBounded` observed `.cancelled` instead of `.resourceLimitExceeded`. The unchanged pressure test reproduced independently after nine focused passes; focused run 10 exited 1 with the same mismatch. The mandatory 20-run aggregate gate therefore remains failing even though the cancellation and keepalive cases passed in the failed aggregate run.
LOGBOOK.md:244:- PHYSICAL RESULT: On the designated arm64 Mac15,9 running macOS 26.5/Xcode 26.5, the rebuilt archive passed 49 inside-out inspection assertions and nine negative drift cases. Ten start/message/stop cycles plus host termination exited 0 with one manager and no provider process; exact-path user-Applications uninstall/reinstall, PlugInKit rediscovery, and a final cycle also exited 0. No probe crash report or privacy finding was present.
LOGBOOK.md:416:- ROOT CAUSE: r4 settled `macos.provider` with **no keychain row and no file exception** because `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` grants `/Library/Keychains` read/write to any process holding `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`. Every observation was taken on **macOS 26.5 (25F71)**. The binding generated-target floor is **macOS 15.0** (`.spec/platform-distribution.md`, ADR-016/3r0993). A base sandbox grant is a property of the **running system**, not of this contract — so the matrix asserted, for nine major releases it never read, something true only of the one it did. Verdict 08 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:435:- FINDING: four more elements carrying obligations were absent from `consumers` entirely, three assigned from inside **prose fields** — `legacy.migrationDecisionOwner` named `TASK-260715-35nc5m` for eight revisions, two sentences named `TASK-260717-xempiv` for five. Hence D1's derived set scans **every string** in the contract, not three named lists.
LOGBOOK.md:478:- ROOT CAUSE: r5 moved eight target rows, two rules, two assertions and the C1 scope, and left `crossPlatformRules[2]` still asserting the **r4** contract — the shared Keychain access group granted to `macos.host`. The r5 validator passed **846 checks** because not one of its 31 rules looked at the summary. The generalisable defect is not the sentence: it is that a summary *asserting* a grant is a **second, unsynchronised statement** of data that lives elsewhere, and nothing in a per-row validator can see the contradiction. Reviewer verdict 05 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:483:- DECISION: the corrected Keychain sentence states the exclusion **universally** ("to NO macOS target on either channel, in either family") rather than naming the macOS host as excluded, which is a deliberate divergence from the reviewer's literal rework text. **Naming a target is what went stale**: r4 named the host as a grantee, r5 moved the row, the name stayed. A universal clause is derived from all eight rows and has nothing to forget. Recorded in the JSON as `whyTheMacOSHostIsNotNamed` rather than left as an unexplained difference.
LOGBOOK.md:484:- ROOT CAUSE (F2, and the more dangerous of the two): r5 corrected `TASK-260728-q5kjta` — the **human ceremony** — and left `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, the **unattended task that spends that authorization**, on the pre-r5 contract. Correcting an authorization node does not correct the node that consumes it. Its AC2 required the packet-tunnel entitlement "**only on provider identifiers**", which contradicted **r1**, not just r5: Apple requires `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension` on the containing app as well as the embedded provider, and R7 has enforced it on all eight target rows since r2. Provider-only verification would have *passed* a portal state in which neither macOS host App ID carries the capability — surfacing at first build, after the sitting was over.
LOGBOOK.md:486:- VERIFICATION: **975 checks** exit 0 (r5: 846); **117 negative gates** exit 0 (r5: 92), 25 new — 18 against F1 including a stale grantee appended *after* an intact grant clause, which a substring check alone would miss; 5 against the P1 pin; 2 against the rationale document. **53/53 r5 preservation assertions** hold against a pre-edit copy: all eight target rows byte-for-byte, exactly two top-level keys added, none removed. `check-legacy-preservation.sh`, `task-board validate`, `swift test` (335 tests / 29 suites) all exit 0. The reviewer's own `jq` gate flipped `false`/exit 1 → `true`/exit 0.
